  "subtitle": "Trace a closed shape on a map and the machine hands you its area — a drafting gesture turned into a computed number",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Planix digital planimeter is the museum's purest 'trace-the-world-into-a-number' input: you run a magnifying tracer lens along a closed outline printed on a map, and a microprocessor integrates the roller/encoder motion in real time to display the enclosed area on an LCD. A hand-drawing gesture becomes a computed figure, with optional RS-232 output streaming results to a computer or printer. It is genuinely distinct from every graphics tablet in the collection (Bit Pad, GrafBar, VersaWriter — all convert a drawn stroke into coordinates) because it computes a *derived quantity* from the traced path as you move, and it is museum's only planimeter. It is the embodied inverse-output companion to the plotter family.",
